# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
# Coverage's database. It was tracked until 2026-10-10: it leaks nothing
# personal - measured, two D:\ paths and no account name - but it is a
# build artifact, and a repository about to be published should not carry
# one.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# MeetAI: secrets - never commit
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
secrets/
*.key
*.pem
token*.json
credentials*.json

# MeetAI: runtime state
var/
logs/
models/

# MeetAI: anything that could hold meeting content.
# Audio containers, including the ones Discord/Opus actually produce, plus
# transcript and subtitle shapes and the JSONL a log sink would write.
*.wav
*.mp3
*.opus
*.ogg
*.oga
*.m4a
*.aac
*.flac
*.webm
*.mp4
*.mkv
*.raw
*.pcm
*.dca
*.srt
*.vtt
*.jsonl
*.transcript

# A single directory for any capture, so nothing escapes via a new extension.
media/

# MeetAI: model weights. Both by extension and by directory, because the
# directory alone was not enough: `git check-ignore` showed a stray
# `weights.gguf` at the repository root was committable, and a multi-GB blob
# is not something to notice in review.
*.gguf
*.safetensors
*.ct2
*.nemo
*.onnx
*.pt
*.pth
*.bin

# MeetAI: benchmark output. Machine-written measurements go under var/, which
# is already ignored; these patterns are the backstop for the ones written
# somewhere else by accident. Results can embed prompts and ASR hypotheses,
# which is content-shaped text, and git history does not forget.
bench*.json
bench*.csv
*-bench.json
benchmarks/out/
benchmarks/*.json

# MeetAI: dataset manifests. Read-speech corpora ship .tsv/.csv manifests that
# contain the reference transcripts; the audio is ignored above but the
# transcript file would not be.
*.tsv
data/

# OS / editor
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/

# MeetAI: speech corpora arrive as archives. The extract is covered by data/
# and var/; the archive landing anywhere else was not. Verified with
# `git check-ignore`: `dev.tar.gz` at the repository root was committable.
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.zip
*.7z

# MeetAI: reference transcripts and ASR hypotheses as plain text. This is the
# worst of the gaps found: VIVOS ships its references as `prompts.txt`, and
# whisper.cpp writes -otxt/-ocsv/-olrc next to the input clip by default. An
# ASR hypothesis of meeting-like speech is content-shaped text, and a
# reference transcript is the thing itself.
*.txt
!*.example.txt
# Pinned benchmark prompts are configuration, not data. A prompt that is
# not committed makes its run unreproducible, which defeats the point of
# hashing it into the artifact. Narrow on purpose: this directory holds
# prompts only, never reference text or hypotheses.
!benchmarks/prompts/
!benchmarks/prompts/*.txt
*.csv
*.lrc
*.ctm
*.stm
*.trn
*.lab
*.TextGrid
*.log

# MeetAI: `benchmarks/*.json` matches one level only. `git check-ignore`
# showed `benchmarks/asr/hyp.json` was committable, and `bench*.json` does not
# catch it because the basename does not start with "bench".
benchmarks/**/*.json
*derived.json
# Exact lock names only. A broad `*.lock` was tried and it swallowed the
# tracked `uv.lock` - caught by tests/test_gitignore.py's second direction.
.bench_asr.lock
.meetai-gpu-benchmark.lock

# MeetAI: scratch directories a careless run would create.
scratch/
out/

# MeetAI: unaudited native blobs. Not a content leak, but a 685 MB binary is
# not something to notice in review and git history does not forget.
*.exe
*.dll

# MeetAI: analysis of a restricted third-party data package. [CHỐT] Sơn
# 2026-10-29: local use only, never pushed. The package ships with a licence
# limiting it to registered parties and forbidding redistribution, so the
# commentary derived from it - design notes and aggregate statistics - stays
# out of the published tree. No file of the package itself was ever copied
# here; this ignores only the note written about it.
docs/research/*-tham-khao-zac.md

# MeetAI: per-machine Claude Code permission rules. Local by definition, and it
# names a push target - this repository is published as a public snapshot, so a
# settings file that reaches the tracked tree reaches the public tree with it.
# .claude/settings.json is NOT ignored: that one is meant to be shared, and
# anything put there is published on purpose.
.claude/settings.local.json
